"The word al-Muzzammil means 'the one wrapped up, the one shrouded.' This title was given because the Prophet (peace be upon him), frightened by the intensity of the revelation he received in the cave of Hira, lay down saying to his wife, 'Cover me, cover me.' Some scholars, however, stated that this title means the one who bears the burden of prophethood. According to Ikrima, al-muzzammil, derived from the root 'ziml', means 'the bearer of a heavy load' and indicates that the Prophet took upon himself this heavy duty.\n\nAllah's address, 'O you wrapped up!' is, according to some exegetes, a severe warning, while according to others, it is an affectionate gesture and an encouragement. According to what Abu Hayyan narrated from al-Suhayli, the Arabs, in order to comfort someone without reproach, would address them with an adjective describing their current state. For this reason, the address takes into account the state the Prophet was in, aiming to awaken him and prepare him for his duty.\n\nThe night prayer (qiyam al-layl) is commanded in this surah. In the initial period, this prayer was made obligatory for the Prophet and his Companions, but it was later lightened. Regarding the duration, an option was offered in the verse by saying 'half the night, or a little less than that, or a little more.' This flexibility provides the possibility of making adjustments according to the length of the nights and the strength of the individual.\n\nReciting the Quran with tartil is commanded. Tartil is to recite by giving each letter its due, distinctly, properly, and reflecting upon the meaning. This encompasses reciting in accordance with the rules of tajwid, with a slow or moderate cadence. The primary goal is to understand the Quran and act upon it. Rising at night and reciting with tartil serves as a preparation for the heavy responsibilities to come.\n\nIn the verse, it is stated: 'We shall send down upon you a heavy word.' This word is the Quran and the duty of prophethood. Just as the descent of revelation was materially heavy, implementing the rulings it contains is also arduous. Night worship is a training to grant the endurance to withstand this heavy duty. The tranquility of the night allows the heart to be more sincere and attentive; for this reason, it has been said that 'the rising by night is more potent and more upright for recitation.'\n\nThe Prophet was commanded to remember his Lord and dedicate himself completely to Him, detached from everything else (tabattul). Tabattul is detaching oneself from worldly occupations and concentrating on worshiping Allah. Afterwards, he was asked to place his trust in Allah, the Lord of the East and the West, to be patient with the disbelievers, and to depart from them in a graceful manner. This signifies a period of patience and forbearance that applied before the command to fight.\n\nAt the end of the surah, the severe punishments prepared for the disbelievers are described: ankal (fetters), jahim (blazing fire), food that chokes, and a painful punishment. Al-Razi interpreted these punishments both physically and spiritually. In the spiritual interpretation, the soul's entanglement in worldly attachments is considered ankal, the pain of being deprived of these attachments is jahim, and being deprived of the divine light is regarded as the greatest punishment.\n\nIn the fourteenth verse, it is recounted that on the Day of Resurrection the earth and the mountains will shake, the mountains will become a heap of poured-out sand, and the surface of the earth will be leveled flat. In this transformation, there is an indication that especially the great mountains will be demolished.\n\nFrom the fifteenth verse onward, the address turns toward the disbelievers. Allah informs them that He has sent them a messenger. This messenger will be a witness over what they do. An analogy is drawn with the messenger sent to Pharaoh (Moses [Musa]). There are three meanings in this comparison: First, to clarify that this is the messenger foretold in the Torah addressing Moses regarding the final prophet; second, to indicate that the polytheists of Mecca are oppressors like Pharaoh; and third, to explain the ultimate outcome by recalling that Pharaoh was dreadfully punished when he rebelled.\n\nIn the seventeenth and eighteenth verses, it is asked how the disbelievers will protect themselves from that terrible day. From the dread of that day, the sky will crack, and children will turn into gray-haired elders. The nineteenth verse states that these warnings are a reminder, and whoever wills may take a path to his Lord.\n\nThe twentieth verse is the verse upon which there is consensus that the command to rise at night given at the beginning of the surah was lightened and modified. According to a narration from Aisha, this verse was revealed eight months after the initial command, turning the night prayer from obligatory (fard) into supererogatory (nafl). According to a narration from Ibn Abbas, however, this command, which had become difficult for the believers, was lightened after one year. There are similar narrations from Qatada and Hasan as well. In some narrations, it is reported that the Prophet worshiped in this manner for ten years, after which it was lightened.\n\nThere is disagreement over whether this verse was revealed in Mecca or Medina. The mention of subjects such as zakah and jihad has been considered evidence that it was revealed in Medina. However, taking into account that zakah was also made obligatory in Mecca, whereas permission for warfare was granted in Medina, the view that at least a portion of this verse may have been revealed in Medina outweighs the other. In conclusion, this verse lightened the duration of night worship, and after the five daily prayers were made obligatory, the obligation of the tahajjud prayer was converted into a supererogatory act.\n\nRegarding the expression 'recite what is easy of the Quran' in the verse, there are three matters: First, is the intent of recitation (qira'ah) the prayer, or merely reciting the Quran? The majority holds the view that the prayer is meant here. Second, the ruling expressed by this command: whether it denotes obligation (wujub/fard) or recommendation (nadb/mustahabb) is disputed. Third is the quantity of the phrase 'what is easy.' While some say at least fifty verses, narrations from al-Imam al-A'zam state that at least one short verse or three short verses must be recited. Imam Malik and al-Shafi'i, on the other hand, stated that reciting Surah al-Fatihah is obligatory. In the continuation of the verse, establishing the prayer, giving zakah, lending Allah a goodly loan (infaq) are commanded, and seeking forgiveness from Allah is recommended."